\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air confined within cavity (E) undergoes thermal expansion, increasing the pneumatic pressure inside the water-filled reservoir (H). This pressure forces water through pipe (L) into the suspended cauldron (M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes the counterweight (R), thereby opening the doors. Once the fire is extinguished, pressure drops, and the system reverts to its initial equilibrium state. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {herondoor}.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Seismoscope developed by Chinese polymath Zhang Heng (132~CE). A central pendulum suspended inside the bronze vessel oscillates in response to seismic vibrations. This displacement triggers an internal lever mechanism that opens the jaws of a specific dragon head, releasing a bronze ball into the mouth of a corresponding frog statue below. Eight radial dragon-frog pairs were meticulously aligned to determine the primary orientation of the earthquake epicenter. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {seismoscope}.}}{3}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Example of a batch-fabricated semiconductor wafer. Parallel manufacturing yields hundreds of identical micro-devices simultaneously, significantly lowering per-unit production costs.}}{4}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Structural representation of a MEMS device. Microstructures typically exhibit characteristic dimensions on the order of micrometers \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {birkholz2013}.}}{4}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Essential elements of an atom-based sensor: a laser light source, an atomic vapor cell, and a photodiode detector. A resonant beam passes through the cell; the transmitted (or fluorescent) light carries information about the atomic quantum state and the physical quantity of interest. Adapted from~\cite {brown2025}.}}{5}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Wafer-scale fabrication of Laser-Written Vapor Cells. Multiple LWVC devices inscribed simultaneously in a single fused-silica substrate by QSENSATO Srl, demonstrating the batch-production capability of the FLICE platform. All subsequent etching, filling, and bonding steps can be performed at the wafer level. Image courtesy of QSENSATO Srl \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {qsensato_web}. }}{11}{figure.caption.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Fabrication worklow of a Laser-Written Vapor Cell. Steps (1-2) describe the FLICE process. In step (4) NEG dispenser placement and bonding are performed. Activation is illustrated by step (5). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lucivero2022}.}}{15}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Laser-Written Vapor Cell manufactured using the FLICE technique. It consists of a cylindrical reservoir (5~mm diameter, 3.5~mm height) connected to a rectangular parallelepiped sensing channel ($1 \times 1$~mm cross-section, 9.5~mm length). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lucivero2022}}}{19}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Hydroxyl radicals formation upon the decomposition of Caro's acid in Piranha solution. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {caro2017}.}}{23}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of a hydroxylated/hydrophilic surface. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {kern1993}.}}{23}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of a contaminant being lifted from a quartz surface due to the Zeta potential. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lin2012}.}}{24}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Effects of plasma treatment on silica surfaces. Plasma removes contaminants and increases the density of available silanol groups, while generating a thin less dense, nanoporous subsurface layer, increasing the diffusivity of water and trapped gases at the interface. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {yu2023}.}}{25}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Bonding energy as a function of plasma treatment duration, highlighting the transition from optimal surface activation to degradation via overetching after \SI {60}{\second } of exposure. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {li2013}.}}{27}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Evolution of bonding energy as a function of annealing temperature. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {li2013}.}}{28}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces Evolution of bonding energy with respect to annealing time. The bond strength follows a logarithmic growth law, where thermal annealing reduces the characteristic time constant $\mu $ to accelerate convergence toward maximum bond strength. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {cho2001}.}}{29}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Effect of applied pressure on the bonding process. Forces perpendicular to the interface suppress void formation by closing residual gaps between surface asperities. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {mai2015}.}}{30}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Pressure plate geometry described by Birckigt et al.\ \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {birckigt2021}. The curved profiles guide contact front propagation outward from the center, reducing the probability of interface void formation.}}{31}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Chemicals employed in the experiment. (1) Deionized Water (DIW) (2) Acetone (3) Ethanol (4) Hellmanex III soap}}{32}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the permeability constant $K$ across uncoated borofloat, uncoated aluminosilicate, Al$_{2}$O$_{3}$-coated borofloat, and Al$_{2}$O$_{3}$-coated aluminosilicate glasses. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {carle2023}.}}{33}{figure.caption.37}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Example of the probe of an Atomic Force Microscope. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wiki_afm_tip}.}}{36}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces 2D Surface images obtained through AFM in three different parts of the pristine Nanoquartz disk's surface.}}{37}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces 3D Surface images obtained through AFM in three different parts of the pristine Nanoquartz disk's surface.}}{38}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Surface roughness distribution obtained through AFM. It can be seen that most of the defects are below the \SI {1}{\nano \meter } range. Furthermore, the mean ($\mu $) is close to zero, confirming that the surface is uniform.}}{38}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Geometrical definition of the contact angle $\theta _C$ at the three-phase boundary between a liquid droplet, the ambient gas, and the solid substrate. The equilibrium value is set by the balance of interfacial energies expressed in Young's equation (Eq.~\eqref {eq:young}). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wikicontact}.}}{39}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Representative contact angle regimes across different wettability states. Surface~A ($\theta _C > 90^\circ $) is hydrophobic; surfaces~B, C, and~D are sorted from partially to fully hydrophilic ($\theta _C \to 0^\circ $). From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wikicontacts}.}}{40}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Examples of Newton rings formed in the interphase of Si/Si wafers. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {nrings}.}}{41}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the razor blade method. A razor blade is inserted between the bonded surfaces in order to quantify the bonding energy between them. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {mack1997}.}}{41}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Real example of the razor blade method. The de-bonded area can be identified by the presence of a Newton ring (light grey area). Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {gabriel2006}.}}{42}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of water stress corrosion at the hydrophilic bonding interface during debonding under controlled atmospheres. Left: wet atmosphere showing ambient water attack on Si--O--Si bonds. Center: anhydrous (dry) atmosphere with reduced water availability. Right: vacuum conditions with partial desorption of interfacial water. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {takeuchi2022}.}}{43}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Schematic diagram of the tensile test setup for bonding strength measurement. The bonded wafer pair is glued to metal fixtures and subjected to perpendicular tensile force. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {gosele2006}.}}{44}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Energy level diagram showing the D1 and D2 transitions in an alkali metal atom. From \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {shi2019}}}{46}{figure.caption.60}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic layout of a radio-frequency OPM. The alkali vapor is spin-polarized along the static field ($B_{\mathrm {DC}}$) by a $\sigma ^+$ pump beam. An orthogonal AC magnetic field excites the Larmor precession, which rotates the polarization plane of a perpendicular, linearly polarized probe beam. This optical rotation is detected by a polarimeter and analyzed using a lock-in amplifier. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {deans2021}.}}{50}{figure.caption.61}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces OPM imaging of (a) a \SI {37}{\milli \meter }$\times $\SI {37}{\milli \meter } square, (b) an isosceles triangle with one side of \SI {37}{\milli \meter } and two sides of \SI {30}{\milli \meter }, and (c) a disk with a \SI {37}{\milli \meter } diameter. Adapted from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {wickenbrock2014}.}}{51}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Cross-sectional schematic of a standard railway track, emphasizing the rail foot region susceptible to atmospheric corrosion.}}{52}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Geometric configuration of the 2D COMSOL simulation domain, indicating the excitation coil position, the corrosion zone at the rail foot, and the mandatory clearance line above which the OPM sensor must operate.}}{53}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces 2D COMSOL maps of the total magnetic field $B_{\mathrm {tot}}$ for both structural conditions at the optimal excitation frequency.}}{54}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Pointwise differential field map $\Delta B_{\mathrm {2D}} = |B_{\mathrm {healthy}} - B_{\mathrm {corroded}}|$ over the full simulation domain at the optimal frequency. The spatial extent of the corrosion signature and the field enhancement in the vicinity of the defect are clearly visible.}}{54}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Differential magnetic field amplitude $\Delta B = |B_{\mathrm {healthy}} - B_{\mathrm {corroded}}|$ at cutpoints $P_1$, $P_2$, and $P_3$ as a function of excitation frequency. The horizontal dashed line marks the QuSpin noise floor of $3\,\mathrm {pT}$.}}{55}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Experimental setup used to detect the ground-state Hanle resonance in a cesium vapor cell.}}{57}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Lorentzian profiles for different DC field configurations: (a)~$B_y$ varied with $B_z = 0$, (b)~$B_z$ varied with $B_y = 0$, and (c)~$B_y$ and $B_z$ varied simultaneously.}}{58}{figure.caption.74}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Raw data and Lorentzian fit of the ground-state Hanle resonance for the optimal DC field configuration ($B_y = \SI {0}{\nano \tesla }$, $B_z = \SI {4815}{\nano \tesla }$). The resonance amplitude is \SI {42.2}{\milli \volt }, the FWHM is \SI {8726.3}{\nano \tesla }, and the derived spin coherence lifetime is $\tau \approx \SI {0.52}{\milli \second }$.}}{58}{figure.caption.75}%
